\lispPackageChapter{chorus}

Load this package with \texttt{(ql:quickload :chorus)}. It reexports the
generic backend interface from \texttt{chorus/backend}, the kernel language
from \texttt{chorus/lang}, the CUDA API from \texttt{chorus/api}, and two
driver variables from \texttt{chorus/driver-api}: \texttt{*show-messages*}
and \texttt{*sdk-not-found*}.

\texttt{*backend*} and \texttt{list-devices} are the calls most programs
make. \texttt{chorus/cuda} is the native CUDA package.
\texttt{chorus/apple-silicon} is the native Apple silicon package.

\texttt{*sdk-not-found*} is true when the CUDA driver library does not load.
Driver calls then signal \texttt{sdk-not-found-error}. Chorus also targets
Apple silicon and AMD GPUs.

The packages below are the ones \texttt{chorus} is built from. Use them when
you want a narrower set of symbols than the reexport.

\section{Components}

The system file \texttt{chorus.asd} defines one module, named
\texttt{source}. Each Lisp file in that module is a component. The
component's name is its path under \texttt{source}, without the type.
ASDF resolves a \texttt{:depends-on} name among those components.

A component depends on another when its file uses a definition from that
file. The use may be a package the file enters or reexports, a macro the
file expands, a foreign type the file names, or a function, variable, or
constant the file refers to. The figures draw those dependencies as trees.
A file stands to the left of the files it depends on. An arrow points at
the file loaded first. A file that several others depend on appears once
beside each of them. A shaded box is a file of the group in the caption.
A white box is a file from another directory, included so the arrow has
a target.

